<p class="eyebrow">A small model about very large fortunes</p>

# Merit or Math?

<p class="dek">In June 2026, Elon Musk's estimated net worth crossed one trillion dollars, on paper. Before I ask what that says about genius, effort, luck, or power, I want to do something deliberately rude: remove almost everything that makes a real life real.</p>

## The headline

Here is the small, strange fact that started this draft:
[a reported estimate of Musk's net worth crossed $1 trillion](https://www.cbsnews.com/news/elon-musk-spacex-ipo-trillionaire-wealth/).
Not cash in a vault. Not a pile of gold you could trip over. Mostly priced shares,
options, and expectations, all moving with markets. Still: a trillion dollars is the kind
of number that makes ordinary comparisons break.

The familiar story reaches for the word **merit**. A person did more, risked more, built
more, saw further. Maybe. Real fortunes are tangled in work, timing, ownership,
institutions, bargaining power, public infrastructure, family, law, luck, and a thousand
things this essay is not ready to pretend away.

So I will pretend them away carefully.

I do not want to begin by deciding whether the headline is deserved. I want to ask a
narrower question: if we strip an economy down until almost nothing remains, can a fair
process still make one person tower over everyone else?

That is the math question hiding behind the merit story.

## First, simplify the cow

There is an old joke about scientists and a cow.

A farmer asks a chemist and a physicist to explain why a cow behaves the way it does. The
chemist comes back with pages of biology: digestion, hormones, grass, microbes, milk,
muscle, heat. The physicist draws one circle.

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p><strong>First, assume a spherical cow in a vacuum.</strong></p>
</aside>

It is a ridiculous sentence, which is why it survives. It is also a useful sentence,
which is the annoying part.

The point is not that cows are circles. The point is that sometimes you remove detail on
purpose. Not because the detail is fake. Not because it does not matter. Because you want
to see whether one mechanism can already explain the thing you are staring at.

A simple model is a bargain. You get clarity. You pay with realism.

So the honest question is not, "Is the model complete?" It is not. The honest question is,
"What can this stripped-down model show before reality gets its hands back on the wheel?"

## Now do it to wealth

Take the headline. Take the person in it. Now remove biography.

Remove the companies, employees, investors, subsidies, contracts, courts, supply chains,
politics, inheritance, risk tolerance, talent, taste, sleep schedule, public image,
management style, and every argument people enjoy having about whether any of it counts
as merit.

What remains is almost offensively small:

<aside class="claim-note">
  <p><strong>You are your net worth.</strong></p>
</aside>

I do not believe that sentence. I want it to feel wrong. The wrongness is the point.

The model I am about to build is not a theory of a human being. It is a controlled little
room where every person starts equal, every selected pair faces a fair coin, and the only
state variable is wealth.

If concentration appears even there, then we have learned something uncomfortable. Not
that merit is fake. Not that the model explains Musk. Only this: local fairness is not
the same thing as global equality.

Next, we build the room.
